00:01:06.570 --> 00:01:09.320
So first I want to
start off with looking

00:01:09.320 --> 00:01:12.770
at just traditional Spark
and Hadoop clusters.

00:01:12.770 --> 00:01:15.230
A lot of Spark and Hadoop
clusters, when people run them,

00:01:15.230 --> 00:01:16.800
are monolithic.

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:19.340
So they get a set of compute.

00:01:19.340 --> 00:01:21.770
They get some implementation
of Spark and Hadoop.

00:01:21.770 --> 00:01:24.567
They slap it on the
hardware they requested.

00:01:24.567 --> 00:01:26.150
And that hardware
could be bare metal.

00:01:26.150 --> 00:01:26.905
It could be VMs.

00:01:26.905 --> 00:01:28.820
It could be kind of whatever.

00:01:28.820 --> 00:01:31.880
Often we see that those
clusters are off by themselves.

00:01:31.880 --> 00:01:33.740
There may be point integrations.

00:01:33.740 --> 00:01:35.900
People run into the
challenges of trying

00:01:35.900 --> 00:01:38.250
to balance that cluster,
so a configuration,

00:01:38.250 --> 00:01:40.130
it's sort of a butterfly
flaps its wings

00:01:40.130 --> 00:01:42.710
over here, that causes
trouble over here.

00:01:42.710 --> 00:01:44.480
A small configuration
change, you then

00:01:44.480 --> 00:01:47.390
have to think about how it
might impact the whole cluster.

00:01:47.390 --> 00:01:49.250
People have tenancy
problems, either

00:01:49.250 --> 00:01:51.980
in terms of their security
model, their resource

00:01:51.980 --> 00:01:54.290
utilization, their
configuration.

00:01:54.290 --> 00:01:56.960
It really ends up being a
bit of a frustrating mess

00:01:56.960 --> 00:01:59.360
that people are just
babysitting their cluster

00:01:59.360 --> 00:02:01.670
and paying less time
to actually getting

00:02:01.670 --> 00:02:03.500
value out of Spark and Hadoop.

00:02:03.500 --> 00:02:06.020
And to be honest, we see
it turns a lot of people

00:02:06.020 --> 00:02:08.250
into really frustrated
users of Spark and Hadoop,

00:02:08.250 --> 00:02:10.474
which is unfortunate,
because they end up

00:02:10.474 --> 00:02:12.140
kind of hating the
tools because there's

00:02:12.140 --> 00:02:14.570
so much just pain involved
in managing their clusters,

00:02:14.570 --> 00:02:15.080
which sucks.

00:02:15.080 --> 00:02:16.460
Like, it shouldn't be that way.

00:02:16.460 --> 00:02:18.710
You should actually like the
open source you're using,

00:02:18.710 --> 00:02:21.650
not be beholden to it.

00:02:21.650 --> 00:02:24.720
So this is what I often
refer to as the devalue chain

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:25.850
of big data.

00:02:25.850 --> 00:02:29.120
When people use open source,
you get this hardware.

00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:31.310
You then have to configure it.

00:02:31.310 --> 00:02:34.372
Cloud has done a good job of
removing some of this pain.

00:02:34.372 --> 00:02:36.830
So instead of needing to rack
servers and configuring them,

00:02:36.830 --> 00:02:37.830
you might just get a VM.

00:02:37.830 --> 00:02:38.870
And that's great.

00:02:38.870 --> 00:02:41.480
But even then, you have some
open source some implementation

00:02:41.480 --> 00:02:43.770
or a proprietary implementation
of Spark and Hadoop.

00:02:43.770 --> 00:02:46.330
You then needs to
tune it, configure it.

00:02:46.330 --> 00:02:48.217
It is a really, really
frustrating experience

00:02:48.217 --> 00:02:49.050
for a lot of people.

00:02:49.050 --> 00:02:51.110
We see a lot of time
spent in people trying

00:02:51.110 --> 00:02:53.150
to manage their clusters.

00:02:53.150 --> 00:02:55.010
A lot of people that
we run into think

00:02:55.010 --> 00:02:56.900
they also need to
do this because it

00:02:56.900 --> 00:02:59.600
becomes a part of how
they've operated for so long.

00:02:59.600 --> 00:03:03.562
So they've gotten used to
administrating their cluster.

00:03:03.562 --> 00:03:05.270
And there's a core
set of tasks that they

00:03:05.270 --> 00:03:06.320
think they have to do.

00:03:06.320 --> 00:03:09.236
And when we try and peel
that away, they don't.

00:03:09.236 --> 00:03:10.610
And a lot of time
that they could

00:03:10.610 --> 00:03:13.490
have dedicated on writing
Spark to go look at data,

00:03:13.490 --> 00:03:17.270
or backfilling old data
to find new insights,

00:03:17.270 --> 00:03:20.420
or anything that's higher value
than sitting on their cluster,

00:03:20.420 --> 00:03:22.170
they're not doing
because they're spending

00:03:22.170 --> 00:03:23.510
their time on their cluster.

00:03:23.510 --> 00:03:24.676
Leads to a whole bunch pain.

00:03:24.676 --> 00:03:26.840
So scaling, even in
a cloud environment,

00:03:26.840 --> 00:03:30.050
can be really tricky
and really painful.

00:03:30.050 --> 00:03:33.020
Utilization-- unless you're
running ephemeral Spark

00:03:33.020 --> 00:03:35.660
and Hadoop, utilization is
a bit of a moving target.

00:03:35.660 --> 00:03:38.014
So you're trying
to plan out both

00:03:38.014 --> 00:03:40.430
how much you think you need
right now-- which can actually

00:03:40.430 --> 00:03:42.055
just be tricky-- and
how much you think

00:03:42.055 --> 00:03:43.760
you might need in the future.

00:03:43.760 --> 00:03:46.092
So unless you're using
ephemeral Spark and Hadoop,

00:03:46.092 --> 00:03:48.050
you're trying to match
how much you're spending

00:03:48.050 --> 00:03:49.250
with how much you're using.

00:03:49.250 --> 00:03:52.130
And again, you're
performing more accounting

00:03:52.130 --> 00:03:54.560
at that point and sort
of less engineering,

00:03:54.560 --> 00:03:58.120
or less technology.

00:03:58.120 --> 00:04:01.210
We really think that the cloud
should make just focusing

00:04:01.210 --> 00:04:02.710
on your data much easier.

00:04:02.710 --> 00:04:04.990
So instead of focusing
on the cluster itself,

00:04:04.990 --> 00:04:06.920
we want you to focus
on the technology.

00:04:06.920 --> 00:04:10.780
So you don't want to focus on
your Spark and Hadoop cluster.

00:04:10.780 --> 00:04:12.620
You want to focus
on Spark and Hadoop.

00:04:12.620 --> 00:04:15.310
And that's really why we went
out and design and continue

00:04:15.310 --> 00:04:16.829
to invest in Cloud Dataproc.

00:04:16.829 --> 00:04:19.120
So if you like
using Apache Spark,

00:04:19.120 --> 00:04:21.399
and you think is often
new features in something

00:04:21.399 --> 00:04:24.267
like Spark 2.3, which
there absolutely are,

00:04:24.267 --> 00:04:26.350
we want you to focus on
those awesome new features

00:04:26.350 --> 00:04:28.183
and the value they might
bring to your data,

00:04:28.183 --> 00:04:31.060
not how you have to think about
when you can upgrade to Spark

00:04:31.060 --> 00:04:32.920
2.3 on your cluster,
or how you're

00:04:32.920 --> 00:04:35.620
going to try and fit it into
your existing production

00:04:35.620 --> 00:04:38.680
workflows.

00:04:38.680 --> 00:04:40.930
So with Cloud Dataproc,
and if anybody

00:04:40.930 --> 00:04:43.955
has seen past presentations,
this is a familiar phrase.

00:04:43.955 --> 00:04:45.580
And it's a familiar
phrase because it's

00:04:45.580 --> 00:04:46.829
sort of our mission statement.

00:04:46.829 --> 00:04:50.722
And it continues to underpin
our thinking and new features

00:04:50.722 --> 00:04:52.180
that we develop
for Cloud Dataproc.

00:04:52.180 --> 00:04:54.770
We want the Spark
and Hadoop ecosystem.

00:04:54.770 --> 00:04:57.940
And when I say that, that's
the whole bucket of components.

00:04:57.940 --> 00:04:59.710
There's a lot that
lives in there.

00:04:59.710 --> 00:05:04.150
There's HBase and
Tez and Impala.

00:05:04.150 --> 00:05:06.800
So I often abbreviate it, saying
Spark and Hadoop ecosystem.

00:05:06.800 --> 00:05:08.800
But it's really that
whole big thing.

00:05:08.800 --> 00:05:10.720
And some of it is,
well, most of it's

00:05:10.720 --> 00:05:12.294
in the Apache
Software Foundation.

00:05:12.294 --> 00:05:13.585
But a lot of it's also related.

00:05:13.585 --> 00:05:15.040
It's privy things like Presto.

00:05:15.040 --> 00:05:17.130
So just as a note,
when I say that,

00:05:17.130 --> 00:05:19.360
I sort of mean I'm speaking
to the whole ecosystem.

00:05:19.360 --> 00:05:21.580
And that's where Dataproc plays.

00:05:21.580 --> 00:05:23.360
Fast, easy, and cost effective.

00:05:23.360 --> 00:05:27.600
So realistically, we do want
you to spend less to do more.

00:05:27.600 --> 00:05:29.430
Sales people love
me for saying this,

00:05:29.430 --> 00:05:32.290
but when you're using
Spark and Hadoop,

00:05:32.290 --> 00:05:34.450
save your money for
high value stuff.

00:05:34.450 --> 00:05:37.900
If you have a Spark job, pay
us for the compute resources

00:05:37.900 --> 00:05:40.240
and for the management
to go run that Spark job.

00:05:40.240 --> 00:05:43.300
And then don't use the resources
when you don't have to.

00:05:43.300 --> 00:05:44.530
Use them for something else.

00:05:44.530 --> 00:05:48.041
Hire more people to look at
your data, or throw a party,

00:05:48.041 --> 00:05:48.540
whatever.

00:05:48.540 --> 00:05:49.414
We don't really care.

00:05:49.414 --> 00:05:52.580
We just don't want you to
waste your time, your energy,

00:05:52.580 --> 00:05:55.690
and your sanity.

00:05:55.690 --> 00:06:00.700
When you look at sort of from
fully DIY to Cloud Dataproc,

00:06:00.700 --> 00:06:02.410
there's a few differences.

00:06:02.410 --> 00:06:04.810
Obviously, when we interact
with a lot of customers

00:06:04.810 --> 00:06:06.460
at the beginning
of their journey,

00:06:06.460 --> 00:06:08.230
they're running
their own managed

00:06:08.230 --> 00:06:11.477
clusters, sometimes in a
cloud, sometimes on premise.

00:06:11.477 --> 00:06:13.060
And they're taking
on the full burden.

00:06:13.060 --> 00:06:14.570
So they're managing
their hardware.

00:06:14.570 --> 00:06:15.820
They're managing the software.

00:06:15.820 --> 00:06:18.220
They're managing
the configuration.

00:06:18.220 --> 00:06:21.836
They may bring in a vendor to
help with the Hadoop stack,

00:06:21.836 --> 00:06:22.960
which is the middle column.

00:06:22.960 --> 00:06:26.840
So the vendor can help pre-tune
some of the components.

00:06:26.840 --> 00:06:29.180
They will create
builds for them.

00:06:29.180 --> 00:06:30.430
They'll often provide support.

00:06:30.430 --> 00:06:32.138
But there's still a
lot that the customer

00:06:32.138 --> 00:06:33.790
is responsible for managing.

00:06:33.790 --> 00:06:35.350
With Cloud Dataproc,
we want to focus

00:06:35.350 --> 00:06:38.549
on your code and your
data, and not other things.

00:06:38.549 --> 00:06:40.840
That's not to say we want to
abstract away your ability

00:06:40.840 --> 00:06:45.640
to do things like custom tune
Spark, or inject new libraries,

00:06:45.640 --> 00:06:46.990
or do really advanced things.

00:06:46.990 --> 00:06:49.930
Dataproc as a
Google Cloud Service

00:06:49.930 --> 00:06:52.090
stands out because
it's really knobby.

00:06:52.090 --> 00:06:54.100
You'll find a lot of
Google Cloud's services

00:06:54.100 --> 00:06:55.360
are not knobby.

00:06:55.360 --> 00:06:58.420
On purpose, Dataproc is
a very knobby service.

00:06:58.420 --> 00:07:00.670
Our philosophy is we
want it to be knobby.

00:07:00.670 --> 00:07:03.472
We want to preset those knobs
to the best of our ability.

00:07:03.472 --> 00:07:04.930
And you'll see some
of the features

00:07:04.930 --> 00:07:07.270
that we're working on
today to add new knobs

00:07:07.270 --> 00:07:09.110
and preset those for you.

00:07:09.110 --> 00:07:12.790
But we want you to be able
to override, to configure,

00:07:12.790 --> 00:07:16.174
to tweak, but not have to think
about it if you don't want to.

00:07:16.174 --> 00:07:17.590
Because most of
the time, you just

00:07:17.590 --> 00:07:20.050
don't have to think about it.

00:07:20.050 --> 00:07:23.745
Under the hood, Dataproc
has a few different layers.

00:07:23.745 --> 00:07:25.870
I'm going to be describing
this left to right, just

00:07:25.870 --> 00:07:28.240
so people can follow along.

00:07:28.240 --> 00:07:30.190
Everything that
happens with Dataproc

00:07:30.190 --> 00:07:32.650
generally happens
through the Dataproc API.

00:07:32.650 --> 00:07:34.750
And that's not to say
that all interactions have

00:07:34.750 --> 00:07:36.458
to happen through the
Cloud Dataproc API,

00:07:36.458 --> 00:07:39.190
it's just how many
happen to work.

00:07:39.190 --> 00:07:40.990
So all of our client
tooling, whether it's

00:07:40.990 --> 00:07:43.270
the cloud console, or
the command line SDK,

00:07:43.270 --> 00:07:45.520
they're all talking
Dataproc API.

00:07:45.520 --> 00:07:50.410
There's endpoints for clusters,
jobs, operations, and now

00:07:50.410 --> 00:07:53.980
very recently, workflows, where
you can specify a workflow that

00:07:53.980 --> 00:07:55.622
will allow you to
create a cluster,

00:07:55.622 --> 00:07:57.580
execute a graph of jobs,
and delete the cluster

00:07:57.580 --> 00:07:59.290
in one operation.

00:07:59.290 --> 00:08:02.830
That API will talk to Dataproc
clusters themselves, which are

00:08:02.830 --> 00:08:05.260
built on top of Compute Engine.

00:08:05.260 --> 00:08:07.942
Dataproc clusters have N
number of master nodes,

00:08:07.942 --> 00:08:09.400
depending on whether
you're running

00:08:09.400 --> 00:08:11.290
just standard
availability, which is one,

00:08:11.290 --> 00:08:13.780
or high availability,
which is three.

00:08:13.780 --> 00:08:17.350
Those nodes then run
Debian Jessie image,

00:08:17.350 --> 00:08:19.630
with our open source
stack, which we use Apache

00:08:19.630 --> 00:08:22.580
Bigtop to build on top of it.

00:08:22.580 --> 00:08:25.151
You can install additional
components if you want to.

00:08:25.151 --> 00:08:27.400
Likewise, since Dataproc is
running on Compute Engine,

00:08:27.400 --> 00:08:31.030
you can do things like SSH
into the VMs if you so choose.

00:08:31.030 --> 00:08:33.549
We also inherit all of the
power and functionalities

00:08:33.549 --> 00:08:37.419
of Compute Engine, so
networks, GPUs, the ability

00:08:37.419 --> 00:08:40.426
to create clusters without
public IPs attached to them,

00:08:40.426 --> 00:08:42.175
all of that is inherently
part of Dataproc

00:08:42.175 --> 00:08:45.190
because we're built
on Compute Engine.

00:08:45.190 --> 00:08:46.460
You'll also see HDFS.

00:08:46.460 --> 00:08:48.520
And HDFS is a bit of
an interesting odd duck

00:08:48.520 --> 00:08:50.800
here because when you
created Dataproc cluster,

00:08:50.800 --> 00:08:52.840
we do provision HDFS.

00:08:52.840 --> 00:08:54.850
The goal is that you'd
only use that HDFS

00:08:54.850 --> 00:08:58.490
for temporary operations, so
splitting, spilling, shuffling,

00:08:58.490 --> 00:09:02.020
but you wouldn't store
data on HDFS persistently.

00:09:02.020 --> 00:09:04.930
And the reason being is we
have a cloud storage connector

00:09:04.930 --> 00:09:08.830
that's Hadoop compatible
file system compliant, which

00:09:08.830 --> 00:09:13.480
allows you to use Cloud storage
as HDFS drop in replacement

00:09:13.480 --> 00:09:17.590
without having to run your
cluster 24/7 just to run HDFS.

00:09:17.590 --> 00:09:20.110
So when a lot people
moved to Cloud Dataproc,

00:09:20.110 --> 00:09:22.450
the very first sort of
big savings for them

00:09:22.450 --> 00:09:24.700
is just not running a
cluster all the time

00:09:24.700 --> 00:09:26.740
to maintain their data.

00:09:30.160 --> 00:09:33.359
Over the years there's been a
set of common pieces of advice

00:09:33.359 --> 00:09:34.900
we've given customers
to try and help

00:09:34.900 --> 00:09:38.410
them get onto Cloud Dataproc
as sort of painlessly

00:09:38.410 --> 00:09:41.770
and effortlessly as possible.

00:09:41.770 --> 00:09:44.260
First, separate
storage and compute.

00:09:44.260 --> 00:09:46.840
And this goes to
my quip about HDFS.

00:09:46.840 --> 00:09:50.080
So to the extent that you can
separate your data at rest

00:09:50.080 --> 00:09:54.460
from your computation, you'll
save, obviously, effort.

00:09:54.460 --> 00:09:55.960
You'll save money
because you won't

00:09:55.960 --> 00:09:59.480
have to maintain the compute
needed just to store data.

00:09:59.480 --> 00:10:02.510
It also allows you to do an
incremental move over time.

00:10:02.510 --> 00:10:04.510
So if you separate out
your storage and compute,

00:10:04.510 --> 00:10:07.170
you can do something like move
just a portion of your data

00:10:07.170 --> 00:10:08.260
to a cloud.

00:10:08.260 --> 00:10:09.640
You can experiment on it.

00:10:09.640 --> 00:10:12.760
You can move maybe just a
few jobs to Cloud Dataproc,

00:10:12.760 --> 00:10:14.890
see how it works,
iterate, rinse and repeat.

00:10:14.890 --> 00:10:17.110
It means you don't have
to do a wholesale lift

00:10:17.110 --> 00:10:19.420
and shift of your cluster
all at once, because that's

00:10:19.420 --> 00:10:20.710
really difficult to do.

00:10:20.710 --> 00:10:23.840
It means that people are trying
to move a lot of moving parts.

00:10:23.840 --> 00:10:25.780
It's much easier to
do it incrementally.

00:10:25.780 --> 00:10:27.738
When you also separate
out storage and compute,

00:10:27.738 --> 00:10:30.040
it means that if you put
something, some storage

00:10:30.040 --> 00:10:31.750
mechanism, like
Cloud storage, you

00:10:31.750 --> 00:10:34.340
can use it without a Google
Cloud products, like BigQuery.

00:10:34.340 --> 00:10:36.910
You don't have to
think about it.

00:10:36.910 --> 00:10:39.430
Use of ephemeral and
long-lived clusters,

00:10:39.430 --> 00:10:42.940
so Cloud Dataproc is
best used ephemerally,

00:10:42.940 --> 00:10:45.405
because you can create
clusters in under 90 seconds.

00:10:45.405 --> 00:10:46.780
You can delete
them at any point.

00:10:46.780 --> 00:10:48.230
We bill by the second.

00:10:48.230 --> 00:10:51.061
So from a performance
and economic standpoint,

00:10:51.061 --> 00:10:53.185
there's a lot of reasons
to use ephemeral clusters.

00:10:53.185 --> 00:10:54.970
You may not always be
able to, because you

00:10:54.970 --> 00:10:57.370
have Spark Streaming, or
honestly, if you actually

00:10:57.370 --> 00:10:58.960
just want to run a
persistent cluster,

00:10:58.960 --> 00:11:01.930
some people prefer to do that
because the predictability

00:11:01.930 --> 00:11:03.740
of their jobs is unknown.

00:11:03.740 --> 00:11:06.340
They have internal SLAs
where seconds matter,

00:11:06.340 --> 00:11:07.930
whatever the reason is.

00:11:07.930 --> 00:11:10.510
Using a mix of ephemeral
and persistent clusters

00:11:10.510 --> 00:11:13.180
works out for a lot of people.

00:11:13.180 --> 00:11:16.771
Rightsizing clusters,
so open source packages

00:11:16.771 --> 00:11:19.270
are great because they allow
you to do a whole lot of really

00:11:19.270 --> 00:11:20.470
awesome things.

00:11:20.470 --> 00:11:24.961
They're really hard to
use all on one cluster

00:11:24.961 --> 00:11:26.710
because the way that
you need to configure

00:11:26.710 --> 00:11:29.620
the environment, in
terms of the software,

00:11:29.620 --> 00:11:31.270
in terms of the
compute resources,

00:11:31.270 --> 00:11:33.610
maybe in terms of the location,
where your data is just

00:11:33.610 --> 00:11:35.950
located, can all vary.

00:11:35.950 --> 00:11:37.950
So depending on
the type of thing

00:11:37.950 --> 00:11:40.420
that you want to do, maybe
the SLA that you'd have,

00:11:40.420 --> 00:11:43.930
maybe you don't care
if your Java MapReduce

00:11:43.930 --> 00:11:45.020
jobs take forever.

00:11:45.020 --> 00:11:47.990
Maybe your biggest reason
for moving them to cloud

00:11:47.990 --> 00:11:48.830
is to save money.

00:11:48.830 --> 00:11:49.330
It's fine.

00:11:49.330 --> 00:11:51.730
You can create a
small cluster, have it

00:11:51.730 --> 00:11:52.960
finish whenever you're ready.

00:11:52.960 --> 00:11:55.099
You can size it just to
fit your preferences.

00:11:55.099 --> 00:11:57.640
If you have Spark and you want
it to run as fast as possible,

00:11:57.640 --> 00:12:00.670
you can rightsize that
cluster for that use case.

00:12:00.670 --> 00:12:03.130
Create and delete
clusters often--

00:12:03.130 --> 00:12:04.780
the too long,
didn't read version

00:12:04.780 --> 00:12:08.020
is if you don't have a
reason to keep your cluster,

00:12:08.020 --> 00:12:08.760
just delete it.

00:12:08.760 --> 00:12:13.044
Like, please don't give us
money to just run stuff and just

00:12:13.044 --> 00:12:13.710
let it sit idle.

00:12:13.710 --> 00:12:15.140
That's a bad use of your money.

00:12:18.030 --> 00:12:20.309
The jobs API, so when we
meet a lot of customers

00:12:20.309 --> 00:12:21.850
and they're moving
to Cloud Dataproc,

00:12:21.850 --> 00:12:25.710
they're using things like
their own custom interfaces,

00:12:25.710 --> 00:12:30.350
or web-based interfaces
to try and submit Hive QL,

00:12:30.350 --> 00:12:32.890
or [INAUDIBLE]
managed Spark jobs.

00:12:32.890 --> 00:12:35.850
Cloud Dataproc has a jobs API
that sits on top of clusters.

00:12:35.850 --> 00:12:37.830
It allows you to
submit and monitor

00:12:37.830 --> 00:12:40.747
jobs for some of the
more common job types.

00:12:40.747 --> 00:12:43.080
We recommend that people do
it because you can do things

00:12:43.080 --> 00:12:45.150
like stream output in real time.

00:12:45.150 --> 00:12:47.040
The job metadata will
outlive the clusters.

00:12:47.040 --> 00:12:48.705
So if you delete
the cluster, all

00:12:48.705 --> 00:12:51.250
of the information about
your job will still be there.

00:12:51.250 --> 00:12:52.420
It also just makes
your life easier.

00:12:52.420 --> 00:12:54.900
You don't have to think about
web UIs to try and submit jobs.

00:12:54.900 --> 00:12:56.399
You don't have to
think about trying

00:12:56.399 --> 00:12:58.350
to create user
accounts for people

00:12:58.350 --> 00:13:02.250
to be able to go create accounts
and submit jobs on clusters.

00:13:02.250 --> 00:13:04.560
It just functionally makes
your life a lot easier.

00:13:07.470 --> 00:13:10.090
The common advice has
worked out really well.

00:13:10.090 --> 00:13:11.790
But there's a set
of new features

00:13:11.790 --> 00:13:13.770
which are either in
Cloud Dataproc today

00:13:13.770 --> 00:13:17.550
or are launching in the next
few weeks that will hopefully

00:13:17.550 --> 00:13:21.591
make moving to Cloud Dataproc
just faster, and easier,

00:13:21.591 --> 00:13:22.590
and more cost effective.

00:13:22.590 --> 00:13:24.215
And Karthik's going
to run through some

00:13:24.215 --> 00:13:25.530
of those new features with you.

00:13:25.530 --> 00:13:26.740
He's going to run
through some of them,

00:13:26.740 --> 00:13:28.080
but not necessarily all of them.

00:13:28.080 --> 00:13:31.190
So if you are an avid
release note fan,

00:13:31.190 --> 00:13:33.930
Cloud Dataproc does
do weekly releases.

00:13:33.930 --> 00:13:35.460
As a teaser, there
are things that

00:13:35.460 --> 00:13:37.050
are not covered
here that are also

00:13:37.050 --> 00:13:40.470
coming out in the next few
weeks, just to set that up.

00:13:43.660 --> 00:13:45.910
KARTHIK PALANIAPPAN:
