00:18:17.444 --> 00:18:19.860
All right, so the second feature
I want to talk about here

00:18:19.860 --> 00:18:21.960
is Dataproc workflows.

00:18:25.470 --> 00:18:28.185
So with the workflows,
you can specify a cluster

00:18:28.185 --> 00:18:30.000
that you want jobs
to run on, and also

00:18:30.000 --> 00:18:31.300
the jobs that you want to run.

00:18:31.300 --> 00:18:34.290
So this really gets to kind
of using ephemeral clusters.

00:18:34.290 --> 00:18:36.016
Previously, you
would need to manage

00:18:36.016 --> 00:18:37.140
three different operations.

00:18:37.140 --> 00:18:40.170
You need to create a cluster,
run jobs, and then delete

00:18:40.170 --> 00:18:41.160
a cluster.

00:18:41.160 --> 00:18:43.540
Well, with workflows you can
do all of that in one step,

00:18:43.540 --> 00:18:46.554
so really helping with
the automation there.

00:18:46.554 --> 00:18:49.740
All right, I just kind
of went deep dive here.

00:18:49.740 --> 00:18:53.579
With the workflow cluster, you
can either specify a cluster

00:18:53.579 --> 00:18:55.620
that the workflow will
create just for those jobs

00:18:55.620 --> 00:18:58.040
and tear down at the end, or
you can give it a selector.

00:18:58.040 --> 00:19:01.110
So if you want to manage
your own pool of clusters,

00:19:01.110 --> 00:19:03.480
let's say you have several
clusters with the same label,

00:19:03.480 --> 00:19:06.240
then you can ask
the workflow API

00:19:06.240 --> 00:19:09.380
to run jobs on the least
loaded cluster with that label.

00:19:12.410 --> 00:19:13.780
So I'll quickly show you here.

00:19:20.999 --> 00:19:22.290
So this is a workflow template.

00:19:22.290 --> 00:19:26.170
I like to use YAML files
to specify my workflows.

00:19:26.170 --> 00:19:28.040
So this is a pretty
common Hadoop work

00:19:28.040 --> 00:19:30.660
[? legis ?] called TeraSort.

00:19:30.660 --> 00:19:34.295
So I have TeraGen to
generate a terabyte of data,

00:19:34.295 --> 00:19:35.670
