the data, and then

00:19:35.670 --> 00:19:38.430
TeraValidate validates that
it was sorted correctly.

00:19:38.430 --> 00:19:42.000
And you can see that I'm using
[? pats ?] on Google Cloud

00:19:42.000 --> 00:19:45.240
Storage here for the data.

00:19:45.240 --> 00:19:47.889
And then finally at the bottom
of this file, in this case,

00:19:47.889 --> 00:19:48.930
I have a managed cluster.

00:19:48.930 --> 00:19:50.596
And the only configuration
I was setting

00:19:50.596 --> 00:19:52.590
was the number of workers.

00:19:52.590 --> 00:19:55.440
So two is probably not enough
workers for this cluster.

00:19:55.440 --> 00:20:01.970
So I'm going to go ahead and set
it to 100, as just as a guess.

00:20:01.970 --> 00:20:04.160
So I can go ahead
and create this.

00:20:08.610 --> 00:20:10.025
So instantiate from file.

00:20:17.700 --> 00:20:22.180
Cool, so this workflow is
going to create a cluster,

00:20:22.180 --> 00:20:25.122
run the three jobs one-by-one,
and then finally delete

00:20:25.122 --> 00:20:26.080
the cluster at the end.

00:20:26.080 --> 00:20:28.246
So I don't need to manage
those steps independently.

00:20:28.246 --> 00:20:31.545
So as you can see here,
it's creating a cluster.

00:20:31.545 --> 00:20:36.650
All right, and again,
canceling is also simple.

00:20:40.250 --> 00:20:42.950
So if I don't want to actually
wait for this to finish,

00:20:42.950 --> 00:20:49.020
can go ahead and cancel it.

00:20:51.860 --> 00:20:56.360
So pretty neat, helped me get
rid of a lot of [? basch ?]

00:20:56.360 --> 00:20:59.430
scripts, actually.

00:20:59.430 --> 00:21:02.730
All right, so this is the last
feature I wanted to talk about,

00:21:02.730 --> 00:21:03.860
autoscaling.

00:21:07.080 --> 00:21:09.450
So as James was talking
about before, one

00:21:09.450 --> 00:21:12.330
of the real benefits of Cloud
infrastructure is that you can

00:21:12.330 --> 00:21:16.680
provision and delete nodes,
delete VMs on demand.

00:21:16.680 --> 00:21:18.750
But before with
Dataproc, you would still

00:21:18.750 --> 00:21:21.621
have to notice that your cluster
was either underutilized,

00:21:21.621 --> 00:21:23.370
or maybe you had a lot
of work queuing up.

00:21:23.370 --> 00:21:25.411
And so you'd have to
manually resize the cluster.

00:21:25.411 --> 00:21:29.550
But now we have autoscaling.

00:21:29.550 --> 00:21:31.290
So as James was
mentioning, we do

00:21:31.290 --> 00:21:33.120
try to be a more knobby
feature than a lot

00:21:33.120 --> 00:21:34.560
of the rest of Google Cloud.

00:21:34.560 --> 00:21:37.274
So with autoscaling, you
have complete control.

00:21:37.274 --> 00:21:38.940
A lot of these do
have defaults, but you

00:21:38.940 --> 00:21:42.630
can specify things like
the min and max workers

00:21:42.630 --> 00:21:46.170
for primary, substandard,
and preemptable workers.

00:21:46.170 --> 00:21:48.360
You can specify the cooldown
period, so how often

00:21:48.360 --> 00:21:50.205
the autoscale decides to scale.

00:21:50.205 --> 00:21:52.890
There are things like graceful
de-commissioning and several

00:21:52.890 --> 00:21:54.433
other properties.

00:21:54.433 --> 00:21:54.933
Yes?

00:21:54.933 --> 00:21:56.820
AUDIENCE: Is scale
down [INAUDIBLE]??

00:21:56.820 --> 00:21:57.986
KARTHIK PALANIAPPAN: It can.

00:21:57.986 --> 00:21:59.850
So yeah, the question
was can it scale down?

00:21:59.850 --> 00:22:00.766
And the answer is yes.

00:22:00.766 --> 00:22:03.577
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

00:22:03.577 --> 00:22:05.077
KARTHIK PALANIAPPAN:
Say that again?

00:22:05.077 --> 00:22:06.478
AUDIENCE: Physical [INAUDIBLE].

00:22:09.009 --> 00:22:10.050
KARTHIK PALANIAPPAN: Yes.

00:22:10.050 --> 00:22:11.770
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

00:22:11.770 --> 00:22:13.880
KARTHIK PALANIAPPAN:
Yes, so I'll explain.

00:22:13.880 --> 00:22:16.100
So a quick like
little deep dive here.

00:22:16.100 --> 00:22:19.560
The way this works, it is
purely based on Hadoop Yarn.

00:22:19.560 --> 00:22:23.480
So if unfamiliar, Yarn is
the scheduler of Hadoop.

00:22:23.480 --> 00:22:27.410
So if I have 100 nodes
and each has 10 gigabytes,

00:22:27.410 --> 00:22:31.340
then I have 1,000 gigabytes
of virtual Yarn memory

00:22:31.340 --> 00:22:32.970
in my cluster.

00:22:32.970 --> 00:22:36.660
So if I have available memory
from Yarn's perspective,

00:22:36.660 --> 00:22:38.824
that means that I'm
actually overprovisioned.

00:22:38.824 --> 00:22:39.740
I have too many nodes.

00:22:39.740 --> 00:22:41.070
And I can scale down.

00:22:41.070 --> 00:22:43.700
And similarly, if there's
a lot of work queued up

00:22:43.700 --> 00:22:45.724
in Yarn that doesn't have
space to be allocated,

00:22:45.724 --> 00:22:46.640
I have pending memory.

00:22:46.640 --> 00:22:49.107
And that means I need
to scale up the cluster.

00:22:49.107 --> 00:22:51.190
So the autoscaling policy
is purely based on that.

00:22:51.190 --> 00:22:57.320
And so when we're talking
about Spark, Spark as a job,

00:22:57.320 --> 00:23:00.680
as executors exit, then you'll
end up having available memory.

00:23:00.680 --> 00:23:04.310
And so we'll remove VMs
to match that workload.

00:23:07.490 --> 00:23:13.170
So a quick demo here, I actually
love using workflow templates.

00:23:13.170 --> 00:23:15.870
So I just modified my
previous workflow template

00:23:15.870 --> 00:23:17.800
and added some
autoscaling properties.

00:23:17.800 --> 00:23:24.340
So here I have a max of 100
standard workers and 100

00:23:24.340 --> 00:23:27.660
preemptable workers, so a total
of a maximum of 200 nodes.

00:23:27.660 --> 00:23:30.697
And I asked it to
scale every 10 minutes.

00:23:30.697 --> 00:23:32.280
All right, and just
for fun, I decided

00:23:32.280 --> 00:23:34.230
to add in the custom
image we built earlier.

00:23:37.810 --> 00:23:44.470
So similarly here would
be instantiate from file.

00:23:50.380 --> 00:23:53.110
And of course, this would
take several minutes

00:23:53.110 --> 00:23:55.600
to run, actually more
like an hour to run.

00:23:55.600 --> 00:23:57.320
So we're not going to
wait here for that.

00:23:57.320 --> 00:23:58.278
I have run this before.

00:23:58.278 --> 00:23:59.742
And I'll show you the dashboard.

00:24:03.810 --> 00:24:05.060
So this is Stackdriver.

00:24:09.040 --> 00:24:10.810
All right, so there
were three jobs.

00:24:10.810 --> 00:24:14.050
You can kind of see
them on the left here.

00:24:14.050 --> 00:24:15.887
The green right here
is pending memory.

00:24:15.887 --> 00:24:16.970
So this is work queued up.

00:24:16.970 --> 00:24:19.600
And you can really
think this as the amount

00:24:19.600 --> 00:24:21.890
of work remaining in the job.

00:24:21.890 --> 00:24:24.315
So this first stage
here is TeraGen.

00:24:24.315 --> 00:24:25.690
So you can see
this is definitely

00:24:25.690 --> 00:24:27.630
smaller than TeraSort.

00:24:27.630 --> 00:24:29.800
So what happens here
is that initially it's

00:24:29.800 --> 00:24:30.910
very slow to progress.

00:24:30.910 --> 00:24:33.400
You can see that it's
pretty flat on the left.

00:24:33.400 --> 00:24:36.340
But then if you look
over here, the autoscaler

00:24:36.340 --> 00:24:41.650
scales up, which makes the
work go down a lot faster.

00:24:41.650 --> 00:24:43.409
Similarly, TeraSort
is a lot bigger job.

00:24:43.409 --> 00:24:44.950
So there's a lot
more pending memory,

00:24:44.950 --> 00:24:47.080
and again the
autoscaler scales up.

00:24:47.080 --> 00:24:50.410
And so again, the
slope is a lot steeper

00:24:50.410 --> 00:24:52.199
as the work finishes faster.

00:24:52.199 --> 00:24:53.990
And then finally,
TeraValidate, the cluster

00:24:53.990 --> 00:24:55.540
is about the right size.

00:24:55.540 --> 00:24:57.340
And so it's mostly constant.

00:24:57.340 --> 00:24:59.540
And then finally it's deleted.

00:24:59.540 --> 00:25:02.705
So, pretty neat.

00:25:02.705 --> 00:25:03.679
Sorry?

00:25:03.679 --> 00:25:05.140
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

00:25:08.549 --> 00:25:14.409
KARTHIK PALANIAPPAN:
[INAUDIBLE] at the very end,

00:25:14.409 --> 00:25:15.450
I was wondering that too.

00:25:15.450 --> 00:25:19.480
You can see that it kind
of goes up here as well.

00:25:19.480 --> 00:25:21.280
Pending memory is
still non-zero.

00:25:21.280 --> 00:25:23.206
So there is still
actually work pending.

00:25:23.206 --> 00:25:24.580
At the very end,
the cluster just

00:25:24.580 --> 00:25:27.410
gets deleted before the
next cool down period.

00:25:27.410 --> 00:25:30.220
And so that's why it
just drops off there.

00:25:30.220 --> 00:25:32.890
I have one more
kind of neat demo.

00:25:32.890 --> 00:25:35.590
So this one was not done from
a workflow template, which

00:25:35.590 --> 00:25:38.200
is actually why there's like a
few hours where the cluster was

00:25:38.200 --> 00:25:38.950
just sitting idle.

00:25:38.950 --> 00:25:40.400
I actually forgot to delete it.

00:25:40.400 --> 00:25:42.406
So use workflow templates.

00:25:51.640 --> 00:25:56.770
OK, so this workload, I was
adding more and more load up

00:25:56.770 --> 00:26:00.400
to kind of this
point here, and then

00:26:00.400 --> 00:26:02.140
reduced the load throughout.

00:26:02.140 --> 00:26:06.410
So the chart, sorry about that.

00:26:06.410 --> 00:26:08.240
So this is actually
a bit misleading.

00:26:08.240 --> 00:26:11.380
I am still increasing the
workload as it falls down.

00:26:11.380 --> 00:26:13.090
It's just that the
autoscaler scaled up

00:26:13.090 --> 00:26:14.794
as it detected
more and more work.

00:26:14.794 --> 00:26:16.960
So around here, the cluster
is about the right size.

00:26:16.960 --> 00:26:18.850
And then when I add
a ton more work,

00:26:18.850 --> 00:26:20.980
again, the cluster scales up.

00:26:20.980 --> 00:26:22.690
And then finally as
the work comes down,

00:26:22.690 --> 00:26:24.670
it scales back down.

00:26:24.670 --> 00:26:26.790
So that's autoscaling.

00:26:26.790 --> 00:26:27.290
Yes.

00:26:27.290 --> 00:26:30.520
AUDIENCE: Autoscaling
[INAUDIBLE]..

00:26:30.520 --> 00:26:32.020
KARTHIK PALANIAPPAN:
So the question

00:26:32.020 --> 00:26:34.150
was, does autoscaling
only work with workflows?

00:26:34.150 --> 00:26:34.900
The answer is no.

00:26:34.900 --> 00:26:37.640
It's based on Dataproc
cluster properties.

00:26:37.640 --> 00:26:40.060
So in G Cloud, if you ever use
[? dash dash ?] properties,

00:26:40.060 --> 00:26:41.440
you know often for
Spark properties,

00:26:41.440 --> 00:26:42.273
it's the same thing.

00:26:46.420 --> 00:26:48.440
And we will have more
time for Q&amp;A at the end.

00:26:48.440 --> 00:26:50.800
So don't worry too much.

00:26:50.800 --> 00:26:56.040
So a quick recap, I'll go
back to presenting here.

00:26:56.040 --> 00:26:58.380
So we created a custom image.

00:26:58.380 --> 00:27:00.030
We wrote workflow templates.

00:27:00.030 --> 00:27:01.140
We enabled autoscaling.

00:27:01.140 --> 00:27:05.070
And we saw the
cluster autoscale.

00:27:05.070 --> 00:27:06.920
So again, this
really gets to kind

00:27:06.920 --> 00:27:10.390
of us becoming a more
and more managed service,

00:27:10.390 --> 00:27:12.770
really helping you
automate your workflows.

00:27:12.770 --> 00:27:14.480
So with that, I'll
hand it back to James.

00:27:19.030 --> 00:27:22.180
JAMES MALONE: So a few of those
features are there right now.

00:27:22.180 --> 00:27:25.350
Autoscaling's coming
in the next few weeks.

00:27:25.350 --> 00:27:26.950
The feature that I
alluded to earlier

00:27:26.950 --> 00:27:29.392
is also coming in a few weeks,
is selectable components,

00:27:29.392 --> 00:27:31.600
where you can pick and choose
what you want installed

00:27:31.600 --> 00:27:34.870
on your cluster, which should
make it easier to customize

00:27:34.870 --> 00:27:38.440
the cluster to your liking.

00:27:38.440 --> 00:27:41.290
If you are curious and
want to get started more

00:27:41.290 --> 00:27:43.572
with Cloud Dataproc,
there is a few things

00:27:43.572 --> 00:27:44.530
that you can check out.

00:27:44.530 --> 00:27:46.270
There is a set of
migration guides, which

00:27:46.270 --> 00:27:48.472
we're expanding over time.

00:27:48.472 --> 00:27:50.680
Migration guides are written
from the perspective of,

00:27:50.680 --> 00:27:53.387
you already have a monolithic,
or several monolithic Spark

00:27:53.387 --> 00:27:54.220
and Hadoop clusters.

00:27:54.220 --> 00:27:56.950
You want to move
them to Google Cloud.

00:27:56.950 --> 00:27:59.120
There is information in
the migration guides,

00:27:59.120 --> 00:28:01.460
right now about moving your
jobs, and moving your data.

00:28:01.460 --> 00:28:03.043
We're expanding that
soon with a guide

00:28:03.043 --> 00:28:05.380
on how to think about a
security model in a cloud

00:28:05.380 --> 00:28:09.730
environment versus a traditional
on-premise environment.

00:28:09.730 --> 00:28:13.700
The Cloud Dataproc docs always
have our newest features.

00:28:13.700 --> 00:28:16.480
So when we release new features,
we release documentation.

00:28:16.480 --> 00:28:19.150
If you are really curious
to stay up-to-date

00:28:19.150 --> 00:28:21.490
with Dataproc features,
the best thing to do

00:28:21.490 --> 00:28:25.120
is actually just follow the
RSS feed for our release notes.

00:28:25.120 --> 00:28:28.540
We do a weekly release
behind the scenes.

00:28:28.540 --> 00:28:30.642
If you ever look at the
Cloud Dataproc images,

00:28:30.642 --> 00:28:32.600
you'll see that they're
incremented every week.

00:28:32.600 --> 00:28:34.183
And the reason is
is we're continually

00:28:34.183 --> 00:28:36.250
building new images every week.

00:28:36.250 --> 00:28:38.480
So it's a really good
way to stay up-to-date.

00:28:38.480 --> 00:28:40.930
We also have a
Stack Overflow tag.

00:28:40.930 --> 00:28:44.050
The team actually looks
at new questions that

00:28:44.050 --> 00:28:46.885
come to the Stack Overflow tag.

00:28:46.885 --> 00:28:48.760
If you have a question
you think others might

00:28:48.760 --> 00:28:51.010
be interested in that
question, or you think

00:28:51.010 --> 00:28:52.450
there might already
be an answer,

00:28:52.450 --> 00:28:56.180
I strongly encourage you to use
that tag, both just as a basis

00:28:56.180 --> 00:28:58.180
to see if somebody already
has asked a question,

00:28:58.180 --> 00:29:01.960
or just to share your question
so others might be able to find

00:29:01.960 --> 00:29:03.280
that information as well.

